                "India's farm inputs are two industries that share a customer and a monsoon. **Fertilisers are a policy business.** ICRA expects volumes to grow only 1–3% a year; the real market size is the government's subsidy, budgeted at ₹1.71 lakh crore for FY27 against ICRA's estimate of ₹2.05–2.25 lakh crore ‘with an upward bias’ after the West Asia shock. India still imports about a quarter of its urea (10.38 million tonnes in FY26 against 5.65 million in FY25, per Chambal's annual report) and most of its DAP. **Crop protection is a roughly ₹82,000cr industry, about half of it exported** (CRISIL). CareEdge's sample of 24 listed companies grew revenue about 14.5% a year over FY18–FY25, through a 14% fall in FY24 when the world's dealers ran down their stocks; CareEdge expects 6–8% growth in FY27, led by volume, with margins near 17%. CRISIL expects a return to 8–10% only if exports hold and Indian demand recovers, and its own FY26 forecast (7–9%) came in 1–2 points short. **My view: the fertiliser makers' next year is decided in Delhi, by the rabi subsidy rates and the Gadepan-3 terms; the crop-protection makers' next year is decided by the rabi season and by Latin American prices.**",
